Implement user story SKY-002-a: Implement SkyscannerClient class and interfaces

## Story Description
Create src/skyscanner/client.ts exporting SkyscannerClient class, SearchParams and FlightResult interfaces. The client uses global fetch, validates API key, handles HTTP errors and malformed responses, and supports an explicit mock mode via MOCK_SKYSCANNER_API env var.

## Acceptance Criteria
- src/skyscanner/client.ts exports a SkyscannerClient class whose constructor signature is constructor({ apiKey }: { apiKey?: string }); the apiKey is stored as a private field (# prefix or private keyword) and is not publicly readable
- SearchParams interface is exported with exactly: origin: string, destination: string, date: string, adults?: number
- FlightResult interface is exported with exactly: price: number, currency: string, airline: string, departure: string, arrival: string, duration: number, stops: number
- searchFlights uses the Node.js global fetch exclusively — no axios, node-fetch, or other HTTP dependency imported
- When no apiKey is passed to the constructor (or empty string '') and RAPIDAPI_KEY env var is unset or empty, constructing SkyscannerClient throws an Error whose message references the missing key; when both are provided, constructor-provided apiKey takes precedence
- When the API returns a 4xx or 5xx status, searchFlights rejects with an Error that includes the HTTP status code; the Error message must NOT include the apiKey value anywhere
- When the API response body is malformed or missing expected fields, searchFlights rejects with a descriptive parse/validation Error
- When MOCK_SKYSCANNER_API env var equals exactly 'true', searchFlights resolves with a deterministic, non-empty FlightResult[] fixture (at least 2 results, valid shape, varying prices) without calling fetch; when the env var is unset, empty, or any value other than 'true' (e.g. 'false', '1', 'TRUE'), searchFlights performs the real fetch call — real HTTP/API failures are NEVER silently replaced with mock/fixture data





